                "action": "new",
                "title": "memory-supercycle",
                "thesis": "AI scaling drives structurally higher memory content per accelerator; HBM production cannibalizes 3-4x its capacity in conventional DRAM wafers; the three-supplier oligopoly reallocates to HBM, starving conventional DRAM/NAND supply; contract prices inflate (1Q26 DRAM +90-95% QoQ, Jefferies forecasting +40-50% in Q3 and +30-40% in Q4, relief unlikely before 2028); memory makers re-rate from cyclical commodity to strategic allocation businesses with multi-year price-locked contracts. Micron is the only US-listed pure play: FQ3-26 Cloud Memory revenue $13.8B at 83% gross margin (vs $3.4B at 58% a year ago), $22B in non-cancelable HBM contracts sold out through 2026, 5-year price agreements through 2030, yet trades ~9x forward FY27 EPS after a ~22% pullback from its $1,255 high. Falsifiers: DRAM contract prices rolling over, hyperscaler capex guidance cuts, CXMT/Chinese supply ramping faster than expected, price-fixing litigation forcing contract renegotiation. Known near-term risk: SK Hynix Nasdaq listing July 10 may pressure MU via rotation.",
                "rationale": "The canonical structural chain (model scaling -> memory demand -> RAM/HBM price inflation -> memory makers re-rate) is now confirmed by contract-price data and Micron's own P&L, and a 22% pullback plus rich near-term index vol offers a better entry than a month ago. Starting conviction 4/5, sized partially to leave room to add after the SKHY listing clears."
              },
              {
                "action": "new",
                "title": "power-is-the-gate",
                "thesis": "Hyperscaler capex (~$630-750B in 2026, up ~60% YoY) has outrun the electric grid: Microsoft disclosed an $80B Azure order backlog it cannot fulfill due to power constraints, and 23+ GW of data center capacity is under construction. When demand is capped by electrons rather than chips, every marginal capex dollar flows to speed-to-power: gas turbines, grid equipment, on-site generation, high-density power delivery and liquid cooling. GE Vernova (turbines/grid gear, multi-year sold-out backlog) and Vertiv (in-rack power and cooling) are the direct order-book beneficiaries, and this leg is less consensus-priced than memory because it looks like boring industrials. Falsifiers: rapid interconnection-queue relief, hyperscaler capex guidance cuts, turbine/cooling order cancellations or pricing pressure, evidence the equipment backlog is being double-counted across projects.",
                "rationale": "Second-order consequence visible in advance: the binding constraint on the AI build-out has migrated from silicon to power, converting hyperscaler backlogs into multi-year forward orders for generation and thermal-management equipment. Starting conviction 3/5 pending order-book confirmation this earnings season."
              }
